@node LibreClaw Configuration
@section Configuration

@subsection Where configuration lives

LibreClaw reads a single YAML file at startup.  There are three
ways to tell cmacs-libreclaw which file to use.

@subsubsection Option A --- declarative, in your init file

@example
(setq cmacs-libreclaw-config-file "~/.libreclaw/work.yaml")
@end example

The path is expanded and checked the first time
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-start} or @code{cmacs-libreclaw-open}
runs.

@subsubsection Option B --- imperative, one-shot

@example
(cmacs-libreclaw-load-config "~/.libreclaw/work.yaml")
@end example

Same result, but also handles the ``libreclaw is already
running'' case: pass a non-nil second argument (or answer the
@code{y-or-n-p} prompt) and the subsystem is cleanly stopped
and restarted against the new config.

Interactively: @kbd{M-x cmacs-libreclaw-load-config} prompts
for the YAML path with completion.

@subsubsection Option C --- let the default-config generator take over

If @code{cmacs-libreclaw-config-file} is never set,
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-start} calls
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-ensure-config}, which prompts to
auto-generate a minimal default at
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-default-workspace} on first run.

@subsubsection Switching vs. reloading

@itemize
@item
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-load-config} switches to a @emph{different}
file and restarts the subsystem (if requested).  In-memory
session state in the old config is flushed.
@item
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-reload-config} re-reads the @emph{same}
file and fires libreclaw's @code{config-reloaded} signal, so
live edits to the YAML take effect without a full restart.
@end itemize

The YAML schema is owned by libreclaw, not cmacs.  See
@file{deps/libreclaw/docs/configuration.md} for the canonical
reference.

@subsection First-run auto-generated config

You don't have to hand-author a YAML to get started.  On first use
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-start} calls
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-ensure-config}, which:

@enumerate
@item
adopts an existing @file{config.yaml} at
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-default-workspace} if one is already there,
or
@item
prompts you (yes-or-no) to auto-generate a minimal default at that
path --- a single Local stdin/stdout channel, your choice of AI
provider, no podomation, no audit log.
@end enumerate

The generator calls @code{cmacs-libreclaw-generate-default-config},
which uses libreclaw's @code{lc_hatch_*} library API under the
hood, so the schema is always in sync with whatever libreclaw
version you linked against.

To trigger it manually without starting libreclaw:

@example
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-generate-default-config
@end example

Customization knobs:

@table @code
@item cmacs-libreclaw-default-workspace
Target directory (default @file{~/.libreclaw}).
@item cmacs-libreclaw-default-ai-provider
Symbol @samp{claude}, @samp{openai}, or @samp{both}.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-default-prompt
Prompt string baked into the Local channel.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-auto-generate-config
Set to @code{nil} to disable the auto-prompt and force the user to
run the generator explicitly.
@end table

For a richer wizard (Matrix credentials, email channels,
podomation DSL, audit log) use @kbd{M-x cmacs-libreclaw-hatch}
instead.

@subsection Minimal example

@example
agent:
  name: "my-bot"
  workspace: "/home/me/.libreclaw"

providers:
  - name: claude
    kind: claude-code
    command: "claude -p"
    model: claude-opus-4-5

channels:
  - id: local
    kind: local
    prompt: "libreclaw> "

podomation:
  enabled: true
@end example

@subsection auth-source for secrets

LibreClaw's YAML supports @code{$@{auth-source:host:user@}}
indirection in any string field.  At load time it calls out to
Emacs's @code{auth-source} to fetch the actual value.

@enumerate
@item
Store your Matrix token, email password, or API key via
@code{auth-source-secrets-create} (or @file{authinfo.gpg}).
@item
Reference it in YAML by host and user.
@item
The raw secret never touches the YAML file or the hatch buffer.
@end enumerate

@example
channels:
  - id: matrix
    kind: matrix
    homeserver:   "https://matrix.example.com"
    user_id:      "@@bot:example.com"
    access_token: "$@{auth-source:matrix:@@bot:example.com@}"
@end example

@subsection Hot reload

@example
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-reload-config
@end example

Re-parses the file and fires libreclaw's @code{config-reloaded}
signal, which the podomation bridge translates into
@code{on_cm_lc_config_reloaded} for DSL rules.

@subsection Customization (core)

@table @code
@item cmacs-libreclaw-config-file
YAML path.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-auto-start
If non-nil, start on @code{after-init-hook}.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-buffer-name-format
Printf format for generic room buffer names (not used by the
cmacs channel, which has its own formatter).
@item cmacs-libreclaw-my-name
Label for locally-echoed outbound messages (default @samp{you}).
@item cmacs-libreclaw-timestamp-format
Strftime format for message heading timestamps.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-message-hook
Hook run on every inbound message.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-room-added-hook
Hook run when a new room buffer is created.
@end table

@subsection Customization (cmacs channel)

@table @code
@item cmacs-libreclaw-default-workspace
Where the first-run default config lives (default
@file{~/.libreclaw}).
@item cmacs-libreclaw-default-ai-provider
Symbol @samp{claude}, @samp{openai}, or @samp{both}; baked into
the generated YAML as @code{ai.model}.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-auto-generate-config
Prompt to create a default on first run (default @code{t}).
@item cmacs-libreclaw-cmacs-channel-history-dir
Per-project buffer history directory (default
@file{~/.libreclaw/cmacs-sessions}).
@item cmacs-libreclaw-cmacs-channel-sender
Sender component in the session key
@code{cmacs:<project-root>:<sender>} (default @code{$USER}).
@item cmacs-libreclaw-cmacs-channel-buffer-name-format
Printf format for chat buffer names; @code{%s} is the project
basename.
@item cmacs-libreclaw-cmacs-channel-autosave-history
If non-nil, persist on kill/save (default @code{t}).
@end table

@subsection Per-project session persistence

The cmacs channel writes two kinds of state to disk, keyed off
the absolute path of each project root:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Buffer history} --- the visible conversation (all the
@samp{** timestamp sender} org headings above @samp{* Compose})
is written to
@file{~/.libreclaw/cmacs-sessions/<sha>.org}, where @samp{<sha>}
is @code{secure-hash 'sha1 <project-root>}.  Written on buffer
kill and after manual @kbd{C-c C-s}, restored on the next
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-open} for the same project.
@item
@strong{AI session context} --- libreclaw's session manager
persists the model's rolling context under the session key
@code{cmacs:<project-root>:<user>}, in
@file{~/.libreclaw/sessions/}.  This is independent of the
buffer history and is managed entirely by libreclaw.
@end enumerate

Opening the same project twice (even across Emacs restarts)
picks up where you left off; opening a different project gives
you a clean slate with its own separate memory.  To reset one
project, delete the matching @file{<sha>.org} file plus the
corresponding entry under @file{~/.libreclaw/sessions/}.
